%S A373487 1,2,3,5,7,10,11,13,15,17,19,23,28,29,31,35,37,41,43,47,53,59,61,67,
%T A373487 71,73,79,83,89,91,97,101,103,107,109,113,127,131,137,139,143,149,151,
%U A373487 155,157,161,163,167,173,179,181,187,191,193,197,199,203,209,211,215,221,223,227,229,233,239,241,251,257,263
%N A373487 Numbers k such that A276085(k) is a multiple of A003415(k), where A003415 is the arithmetic derivative and A276085 is fully additive with a(p) = p#/p.
%C A373487 Sequence A276086(A380527(.)) when sorted into ascending order gives the {intersection of this sequence with A048103}. As A380527 is a subsequence of A381035, this intersection is by the given correspondence a subsequence of (primes U A080364). Note that this applies also to the third known nonsquarefree term of this sequence, 855523 (= 67 * 113^2), which indeed disproves the conjecture I had made in A380527, while the first two nonsquarefree terms 28 and 33212 are both multiples of 4, and thus outside of the intersection.- _Antti Karttunen_, Jan 28 2026
%C A373487 From _Antti Karttunen_, Oct 23 2026: (Start)
%C A373487 There are many other terms of the form p^2*q after 855523, see A401230.
%C A373487 There are terms of the form p^2*q*r: 278638999, 367794667, etc, see A401371.
%C A373487 There are terms of the form p^3*q, at least this one: 26653647817663 = 2333^3 * 2099, see A401063.
%C A373487 Often such terms have also several multiples that are present too, e.g., for 855523, we also have its multiples 108651421, 267778699, 296866481, etc, see A401055.
%C A373487 (End)
%H A373487 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A373487/b373487.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
